--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_after ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The diagnosis says the leader loses unflushed X, restarts at i-1, and restores leadership from its persisted committed vote, letting it propose Y with \"precisely the same id\" (term T, old leader, index i) as X. That matches the criterion: a different entry at the same index under the same log id as the lost entry."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_the_lo ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The submission says X is one that a follower has persisted before the crash, the leader loses its unflushed X, and it mentions \"replication before the leader's flush completes\". Together this states that the entry was replicated to a follower before the leader's own flush finished, so the crash removed it only from the leader. The wording is somewhat compact, but the content matches."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_the_fo ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The submission says the follower holding X \"sees a matching id and retains X,\" and that \"Matching log ids are supposed to identify identical entries; follower reconciliation relies on that invariant.\" It also says a quorum can commit Y while the first follower applies X, \"producing inconsistent state machines.\" That covers the criterion: an equal id is taken to mean an equal entry, so the follower keeps X and different entries are applied. It does not say outright that no error is raised. That is only a minor gap, since the divergence it describes implies it."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_identify_leader_r ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The diagnosis says that restoring leadership from the persisted committed vote lets the leader propose Y with exactly the same id. It also names \"startup restoring authority without an election\" as a contributing factor. That identifies leader restore as what lets the log id be reused. The wording on \"same term, same leader id, no election\" is spread across the text but all present."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_the_re ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The submission says startup sends a former leader through the ordinary election path in term T+1. It says the leader cannot propose until a quorum grants authority. This matches the criterion: it wins an election for a higher term before accepting proposals, and it does not resume its old term. Its title, \"Restarted leaders must campaign in a new term,\" says the same thing."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_explain_that_the ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The diagnosis says startup sends the former leader through an election in term T+1, and that the new leader's blank entry and later client entries have term T+1. It also says the repair needs \"a fresh log-id namespace\". That means the new term gives a new leader id, so entries written afterwards can't carry the lost entry's id. The point is made in substance, though it never uses the words \"new leader id\"."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_explain_that_a_re ---
{"score": "no", "reasoning": "The submission says a new term is needed and rejects several alternatives: waiting for a timer, comparing payloads, appending an old-term blank entry, and changing the committed bit. It never says that a re-election or quorum probe within the same term is insufficient. It also never says a follower that is down during that round can still hold the lost entry. The closest point is that a new term is needed for a fresh log-id namespace, but that is not the required explanation."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_the_fo ---
{"score": "no", "reasoning": "The diagnosis says the stored vote is never rolled back or demoted. That matches the \"storage never moves backwards\" half of the criterion. But it also says changing the in-memory vote's committed bit would not be enough. And with restoration disabled it says the in-memory committed vote stays intact. It never says the former leader's vote is demoted in memory only. The submitted repair avoids demotion and runs an ordinary election that writes a strictly newer vote. The criterion allows an equivalent mechanism, but this one does not state the in-memory demotion with storage left alone. It reaches monotonic storage by a different route, so I judge it not to match."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_the_re ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The diagnosis says: \"The log storage contract is unchanged: local append completion may still be asynchronous, and replication need not wait for the local flush. No stronger durability requirement is imposed.\" This states the repair keeps appends asynchronous and does not require durability before replication. It doesn't use the exact phrase \"append may return before entries are persisted\", but the meaning is the same."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_with_e ---
{"score": "no", "reasoning": "The submission's disabled-restoration paragraph says startup leaves the stored and in-memory committed vote intact, creates no leadership state, and selects follower or learner from membership. That covers the follower/learner start and the kept vote. It never says the node waits for a normal election. Instead it says \"An explicit election still advances to T+1,\" which isn't the same claim. The statement is only partly covered, so I'm scoring it no."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_the_st ---
{"score": "no", "reasoning": "The diagnosis says startup sends a former leader through the ordinary election path in term T+1. It never says the election starts at once, without waiting for an election timeout. It also never says the election is independent of enable_tick and enable_elect. It only says \"Merely waiting for a timer ... would not establish\" a fresh namespace, which is a different point. The criterion is not met."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_on_win ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The submission says \"the new leader's blank entry and subsequent client entries have term T+1\", and that \"the newly elected leader's blank entry then commits and applies\". It also says the node \"cannot propose until a quorum grants authority\". Together this says the winner appends a blank entry in the new term before client entries. It does not say \"under its new leader id\" in those words, but T+1 is the new leader id's term, so the claim is effectively stated."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_start ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The submission states: \"Nodes that were not leaders follow their existing startup path without a new election or vote rewrite.\" That says startup for non-leader nodes (followers, learners, unfinished candidates) is unchanged. It doesn't list the categories explicitly, but \"not leaders\" covers them."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_state_that_leader ---
{"score": "no", "reasoning": "The submission never says that leader restore stays enabled by default or that disabling it by default is not the fix. It only describes behavior \"with restoration enabled\" and \"with restoration disabled\", and it never mentions the default setting."}

